1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,360
Is that all we are are we just a bunch of traits no we're not remember you're like some other people and like no other person.
我们就只有这些吗？只是一堆人格特质吗？不是。记住，你既像某些人，也有任何其他人都不像的地方。

2
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:19,599
How about that idiosyncratic you as elizabeth or as george.
那独一无二的你呢？作为伊丽莎白，或作为乔治的那个你呢？

3
00:00:19,599 --> 00:00:24,000
You may share your extraversion or your neuroticism.
你也许与别人有相同的外向程度，或者神经质倾向。

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:31,440
But are there some distinctively elizabethan features of your behavior or georgian of yours.
但是，你的行为里，会不会有些特别属于伊丽莎白的特点，或者属于乔治的特点呢？

5
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:36,000
That makes us understand you better than just a bunch of traits.
这些特点让我们更好地理解你，而不只是通过一堆特质认识你。

6
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:42,880
That make us love you not just because you're a certain type of person.
这些特点让我们喜爱你，而不只是因为你属于某一种类型的人。

7
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:51,039
I'm uncomfortable putting people in pigeon holes I don't even think pigeons belong in pigeon holes.
我不喜欢把人塞进分类格子里。我甚至觉得，鸽子也不该被塞在鸽子笼的小格里。

8
00:00:51,039 --> 00:00:59,200
So what is it that makes us different it's the doings that we have in our life the personal projects.
那是什么让我们不同呢？是我们生活中正在做的事，是我们个人投入的项目。

9
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:05,200
You have a personal project right now but nobody really may nobody may know it here.
你现在就有一个个人项目，但这里也许真的没人——也许没人知道。

10
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:13,029
It relates to your kid you've been back three times to the hospital and they still don't know what's wrong.
它可能关系到你的孩子。你已经往医院跑了三次，可他们还是不知道出了什么问题。

11
00:01:13,039 --> 00:01:18,000
Or it could be your mom and you've been acting out of character.
也可能关系到你的母亲，而你的行为已经不像平常的自己了。

12
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,320
These are free traits you're very agreeable but you act disagreeably.
这就是所谓的自由特质：你本来很随和，却表现得不那么好说话。

13
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:33,599
In order to break down those barriers of administrative torpor in the hospital to get something for your mom or your child.
为了打破医院里行政迟缓造成的障碍，为你的母亲或孩子争取到需要的东西。

14
00:01:33,599 --> 00:01:41,510
What are these free traits they're where we enact a script in order to advance a core project in our lives.
什么是这些自由特质呢？就是我们为了推进生活中的一项核心目标，而去扮演某种角色、采取相应的行为。

15
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:46,560
And they are what matters don't ask people what type you are.
这些才是重要的。不要问人“你是什么类型”。

16
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:51,120
Ask them what are your core projects in your life and we enact those free traits.
问他们：“你生活中的核心目标是什么？”我们会为了这些目标表现出相应的自由特质。

17
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:59,520
I'm an introvert but I have a core project which is to profess I'm a professor.
我是个内向的人，但我有个核心目标，就是传道授业；我是一名教授。

18
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:09,520
And I adore my students and I adore my field and I can't wait to tell them about what's new.
我热爱我的学生，也热爱我的领域，我迫不及待地想告诉他们有什么新进展。

19
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:13,440
What's exciting what I can't wait to tell them about.
有什么让人兴奋的事，有什么是我迫不及待想告诉他们的。

20
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:24,000
And so I act in an extroverted way because at 8 in the morning the students need a little bit of humor a little bit of engagement to keep them going in arduous days of study.
所以我会表现得很外向，因为早上八点的学生需要一点幽默、需要被调动起来，才能撑过艰苦的学习日。

21
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:37,120
But we need to be very careful when we act protractedly out of character sometimes we may find that we don't take care of ourselves.
但当我们长时间做出不像平常自己的举动时，得非常小心；有时我们会发现，自己没有照顾好自己。

22
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:45,670
I find for example after a period of pseudo extroverted behavior I need to repair somewhere on my own.
比如，我发现，在一段假装外向的表现之后，我需要独自退到某个地方。

23
00:02:45,680 --> 00:02:54,480
As susan cain said in her quiet book uh in a chapter that featured this strange canadian professor who was teaching at the time at harvard.
正如苏珊·凯恩在《安静》里说的，呃，其中有一章写到这个古怪的加拿大教授，他当时正在哈佛教书。

24
00:02:54,480 --> 00:03:01,990
Um I sometimes go to the men's room to escape the slings and arrows of outrageous extroverts.
嗯，我有时会躲进男厕所，避开那些放肆的外向者射来的“投石与箭雨”。

25
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:10,959
I remember one particular day when I was um retired to a cubicle trying to avoid over stimulation.
我记得有一天，嗯，我退进一个隔间，想避开过多的刺激。

26
00:03:10,959 --> 00:03:18,159
And uh a real extrovert came in beside me uh not right in my cubicle but in the next cubicle over.
结果，呃，一个十足的外向者来到我旁边——不是进了我的隔间，而是进了隔壁那个。

27
00:03:18,159 --> 00:03:22,800
And I could hear various evacuatory noises which we hate even our own.
我能听到各种排泄的声音，我们连自己发出的这种声音都不喜欢。

28
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:28,080
That's why we flush during as well as after.
所以我们才会中途冲水，结束以后也冲。

29
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:38,480
And then I then I heard this growly voice saying hey is that dr little.
然后我——然后我听见一个粗哑的声音说：“嘿，那是利特尔博士吗？”

30
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:48,000
If anything is guaranteed to constipate an introvert for 6 months is talking on the john.
要说有什么一定能让内向的人便秘六个月，那就是坐在马桶上时跟人说话。

31
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:55,800
That's where I'm going now don't follow me thank you.
我现在就要去那儿了。别跟着我。谢谢。
